All six `find()` results are immediately cast with `as MindmapLayoutNode` without first asserting they are non-null. If a node is not found, `child1` (etc.) will be `undefined`, and the subsequent `.section` or `.cssClasses` access will throw a runtime `TypeError` with an unhelpful message, obscuring the real failure. Add an explicit guard before use:
```ts
const child1 = result.nodes.find((n) => n.label === '1') as MindmapLayoutNode;
expect(child1).toBeDefined();
```
This pattern is consistent with how other test files handle potentially-absent DOM/node lookups and produces clearer failure messages.

The test establishes the wrapping boundary but omits CSS-class assertions for `child1` (the pre-wrap baseline) and `child11` (the last valid section before the wrap). Without them the test is an incomplete regression baseline — a future change could silently corrupt section 0–10 CSS classes for nodes below the boundary without any assertion catching it. Consider adding:
```ts
expect(child1.cssClasses).toBe('mindmap-node section-0');
expect(child11.cssClasses).toBe('mindmap-node section-10');
```
